Other scholars such as Walter Scheps and Isabel G. MacCaffrey have noted Middle-earth's "spatial cum moral dimensions",<ref name="Scheps 1975">{{cite book |last=Scheps |first=Walter |chapter=The Interlace Structure of 'The Lord of the Rings' |editor-last=Lobdell |editor-first=Jared |editor-link=Jared Lobdell |title=[[A Tolkien Compass]] |date=1975 |publisher=[[Open Court Publishing Company|Open Court]] |isbn=978-0-8754-8303-0 |pages=44–45}}</ref><ref name="MacCaffrey 1959">{{cite book |last1=MacCaffrey |first1=Isabel G. |title=Paradise Lost as Myth |url=https://archive.org/details/paradiselostasmy0000macc |url-access=registration |date=1959 |publisher=[[Harvard University Press]] |oclc=1041902253 |page=[https://archive.org/details/paradiselostasmy0000macc/page/55 55]}}</ref> though not identically with Magoun's interpretation. In their view, North and West are generally good, South and East evil. That places the Shire and the elves' [[Grey Havens]] in the Northwest as certainly good, and Mordor in the Southeast as certainly evil; Gondor in the Southwest is in their view morally ambivalent, matching the characters of both [[Boromir]] and [[Denethor]]. They observe further that the Shire's four quadrants or "Farthings" serve as a "microcosm" of the moral geography of Middle-earth as a whole: thus, the evil [[Nazgûl|Black Riders]] appear first in the Eastfarthing, while the once good but corrupted [[Saruman]]'s men arrive in the Southfarthing.<ref name="Scheps 1975"/> J. K. Newman compares the adventurous quest to Mordor to "the perpetual temptation felt in the West 'to hold the gorgeous East in fee'" (citing [[William Wordsworth|Wordsworth]] on [[Venice]]), in a tradition which he traces back to [[Herodotus]] and to the myth of the [[Golden Fleece]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Newman |first=J. K. |title=J.R.R. Tolkien's 'The Lord of the Rings': A Classical Perspective |journal=Illinois Classical Studies |volume=30 |year=2005 |pages=229–247 |jstor=23065305}}</ref>

=== Origins ===

{{further|Tolkien's influences}}

[[File:Middle-earth's Geographic Influences.svg|thumb|center|upright=3|Classical, medieval, and recent [[Tolkien's influences|influences on the geography of Middle-earth]]. All locations are approximate.<ref name="Garth 2020">Main source is {{cite book |last=Garth |first=John |author-link=John Garth (author) |title=The Worlds of J. R. R. Tolkien: The Places that Inspired Middle-earth |title-link=The Worlds of J. R. R. Tolkien |date=2020 |publisher=[[Frances Lincoln Publishers]] & [[Princeton University Press]] |isbn=978-0-7112-4127-5 |pages=12–13<!--The Shire-->, 39<!--Harad-->, 41, 151<!--Gondor-->, 32<!--Dwarves-->, 30<!--Trolls-->, 37<!--Numenor-->, 55<!--Elves-->, 88<!--Misty Mts-->, 159–168, 175, 182<!--Mordor--> and throughout}}; minor sources are listed at [[:File:Middle-earth's_Geographic_Influences.svg]].</ref>|alt=Influences on Middle-earth's geography]]

[[File:Idylls of the King 18.jpg|thumb|upright|Tolkien borrowed the [[Arthurian legend|Arthurian]] place-name [[Brocéliande]] for an early version of Beleriand.<ref name="Fimi 2007"/> 1868 illustration by [[Gustave Doré]]|alt=Illustration of legendary medieval forest ]]

Tolkien scholars including [[John Garth (author)|John Garth]] have traced many features of Middle-earth to literary sources or real-world places. Some places in Middle-earth can be more or less firmly associated with a single place in the real world, while other locations have had two or more real-world origins proposed for them. The sources are diverse, spanning [[Tolkien and the classical world|classical]], [[Tolkien and the medieval|medieval]], and [[Tolkien's modern sources|modern elements]].<ref name="Garth 2020"/> Other elements relate to [[Old English poetry]]: several of the customs of Rohan in particular can be traced to ''[[Beowulf and Middle-earth|Beowulf]]'', on which Tolkien was an expert.<ref name="Shippey 2005 OE">{{harvnb|Shippey|2005|pp=66–74, 90–97, and throughout}}</ref>

Some Middle-earth placenames were based on the sound of places named in literature; thus, Beleriand was borrowed from the [[Brocéliande|Broceliand]] of medieval romance.<ref name="Fimi 2007">{{cite journal |last=Fimi |first=Dimitra |author-link=Dimitra Fimi  |title=Tolkien's 'Celtic type of legends': Merging Traditions|journal=[[Tolkien Studies]] |volume=4 |year=2007 |pages=53–72 |doi=10.1353/tks.2007.0015|s2cid=170176739 }}</ref> Tolkien tried out many invented names [[Sound and language in Middle-earth|in search of the right sound]], in Beleriand's case including Golodhinand, Noldórinan ("valley of the [[Noldor]]"), Geleriand, Bladorinand, Belaurien, Arsiriand, Lassiriand, and Ossiriand (later used as a name for the easternmost part of Beleriand).<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Tolkien|1986|loc="Commentary on Canto I"}}</ref> The Elves have been linked to Celtic mythology.<ref name="Fimi 2006">{{cite news |last=Fimi |first=Dimitra |author-link=Dimitra Fimi |title="Mad" Elves and "Elusive Beauty": Some Celtic Strands of Tolkien's Mythology |publisher=Dimitra Fimi |date=August 2006 |url=http://dimitrafimi.com/articlesandessays/mad-elves-and-elusive-beauty-some-celtic-strands-of-tolkiens-mythology/}}</ref> The [[Battle of the Pelennor Fields]] has parallels with the [[Battle of the Catalaunian Plains|Battle of the Catalaunian Fields]].<ref name="Solopova 2009">{{ME-ref|Solopova|pp. 70-73}}</ref> The Misty Mountains derive from the ''[[Poetic Edda]]'', where the protagonist in the ''[[Skírnismál]]'' notes that his quest will involve misty mountains peopled with orcs and giants,<ref name="Shippey 2005">{{harvnb|Shippey|2005|pp=80–81, 114}}</ref> while the mountains' character was partly inspired by Tolkien's travels in the [[Swiss Alps]] in 1911.<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Carpenter|2023|loc=#306 to Michael Tolkien, 1967 }}</ref> Mirkwood is based on ''[[Myrkviðr]]'', the romantic vision of the dark forests of the North.<ref name="Drout2007">{{cite book |chapter=Mirkwood |last=Evans |first=Jonathan |author-link=Jonathan Evans (scholar) |title=J. R. R. Tolkien Encyclopedia: Scholarship and Critical Assessment |title-link=J. R. R. Tolkien Encyclopedia |editor=Drout, Michael D. C. |editor-link=Michael D. C. Drout |publisher=[[Routledge]]|year=2006 |pages=429–430 |isbn=0-415-96942-5}}</ref>
Scholars have likened Gondor to [[Byzantium]] (medieval Istanbul),<ref>{{cite book |title=Tolkien and the Study of his Sources |last=Librán-Moreno |first=Miryam |publisher=[[McFarland & Company|McFarland]] |year=2011 |isbn=978-0-7864-6482-1 |editor-last=Fisher |editor-first=Jason |author-link=Jason Fisher |pages=84–116 |chapter='Byzantium, New Rome!' Goths, Langobards and Byzantium in ''The Lord of the Rings'' |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=98VQ3gHsVsMC&q=Gondor+Byzance&pg=PA98}}</ref> while Tolkien connected it to Venice.<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Carpenter|2023|loc=#168 to R. Jeffrey, September 1955 }}</ref> The [[Harad|Corsairs of Umbar]] have been linked to the [[Barbary corsairs]] of the late Middle Ages.<ref>{{cite book |last=Bowers |first=John M. |title=Tolkien's Lost Chaucer |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Q5OwDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA170 |year=2019 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-258029-0 |page=170}}</ref> Númenor echoes the mythical [[Atlantis]] described by [[Plato]].<ref name="Letters Atlantis" group=T>{{harvnb|Carpenter|2023|loc=#131 to [[Milton Waldman]] c. 1951, #154 to [[Naomi Mitchison]] 25 September 1954, #156 draft to [[Robert Murray (priest)|Robert Murray]], 4 November 1954, #227 to Mrs E. C. Ossen Drijver 5 January 1961 }}</ref>

About the origins of his storytelling and [[Tolkien's maps|the place of cartography]] within it, Tolkien stated in a letter:<ref name="Shippey 2005"/>

{{blockquote|I wisely started with a map, and made the story fit (generally with meticulous care for distances). The other way about lands one in confusions and impossibilities, and in any case it is weary work to compose a map from a story.<ref group=T>{{harvnb|Carpenter|2023|loc=#144 to [[Naomi Mitchison]], 25 April 1954 }}</ref>}}

Writing in ''[[Mythlore]]'', [[Jefferson P. Swycaffer]] suggested that the political and strategic situations of Gondor and Mordor in the [[Siege of Gondor]] were "analogous to [[Constantinople]] facing the boxshape of [[Asia Minor]]"; that "Dol Amroth makes a fine [[Republic of Venice|Venice]]"; that the Rohirrim and their grasslands are comparable to "[[Kingdom of Hungary|Hungary of the Magyars]], who were weak allies of Byzantine Constantinople"; and that the [[Corsairs of Umbar]] resembled the [[Barbary pirates]] who served [[Mehmed the Conqueror]].<ref name="Swycaffer 1983">{{cite journal |last=Swycaffer |first=Jefferson |year=1983 |title=Historical Motivations for the Siege of Minas Tirith |journal=[[Mythlore]] |volume=10 |at=article 14 |url=https://dc.swosu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1687&context=mythlore}}</ref>

The linguist [[David Salo]] writes that Gondor recalls "a kind of decaying Byzantium"; its piratical enemy Umbar like the seagoing [[Carthage]]; the Southrons (of Harad) "Arab-like"; and the Easterlings "suggesting [[Sarmatians]], [[Huns]] and [[Pannonian Avars|Avars]]".<ref name="DriverRay2004">{{cite book |last=Salo |first=David |author-link=David Salo |chapter=Heroism and Alienation through Language in ''The Lord of the Rings'' |editor1-last=Driver |editor1-first=Martha W. |editor2-last=Ray |editor2-first=Sid |title=The Medieval Hero on Screen: Representations from Beowulf to Buffy |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=CB6hBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA26 |date=2004 |publisher=[[McFarland & Company|McFarland]] |isbn=978-0-7864-1926-5 |pages=23–37}}</ref>

=== Geology ===

{{main|Geology of Middle-earth}}

The [[geologist]]s Margaret M. Howes in 1967,{{sfn|Howes|1967|pp=3-15}} Robert C. Reynolds in 1974,{{sfn|Reynolds|1974|pp=67–71}} and then [[William Sarjeant]] in 1992, used the information from the [[Tolkien's artwork|illustrations]], [[Tolkien's maps|maps]], and text of [[J. R. R. Tolkien]]'s fiction, especially ''[[The Lord of the Rings]]'', to create a conjectural reconstruction of [[Middle-earth]]'s geology. They proposed tectonic movements and glaciations to shape the described landscapes.{{sfn|Sarjeant|1995|pp=334-339}} 

The geologist Alex Acks, writing on [[Tor.com]], outlines mismatches between Tolkien's maps and the processes of [[plate tectonics]] which shape the Earth's [[continent]]s and [[mountain range]]s. Acks comments that no natural process creates right-angle junctions in mountain ranges, such as are seen around [[Mordor]] and at both ends of the [[Misty Mountains]] on Tolkien's maps.{{sfn|Acks|2017a}} In addition, Tolkien's rivers fail to behave like natural rivers, forming regularly-branched streams in [[drainage basin]]s demarcated by high ground.{{sfn|Acks|2017b}}

